      "title": "OmniScribble exposes sketch decomposition and motion plans as editable artifacts",
      "summary": "Researchers developed OmniScribble, a mixed-initiative authoring system for animating multi-object sketches. The tool turns AI-generated object decomposition and motion planning into inspectable, repairable artifacts, allowing creators to correct and redirect automated decisions instead of accepting one-shot generations.",
      "key_facts": [
        "Treats object decomposition and motion planning as inspectable, editable artifacts.",
        "Enables mixed-initiative animation authoring for multi-object sketches.",
        "Lets creators repair and redirect AI decisions instead of re-prompting."
      ],
      "design_implication": "Generative motion tools often force creators into an all-or-nothing loop of re-prompting full video outputs whenever timing or segmentation fails. Exposing intermediate steps as distinct, manipulable canvas layers lets designers debug automated motion trajectories directly without starting over. Add editable intermediate state controls to generative tool concepts so users can adjust object boundaries before motion compiles.",
      "implication_lens": "workflow",
      "visual_need": "illustrate",
      "source_name": "arXiv cs.HC",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04284",
      "source_date": "2026-11-04T05:00:00.000Z",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "OmniScribble treats object decomposition and motion planning as inspectable, editable artifacts rather than opaque generation.",
          "quote": "We present OmniScribble, a system for animating multi-object sketches that treats object decomposition and motion planning as editable artifacts."
        }
      ],
