      "title": "Agentic Path Maps reveal unchosen decision branches after agent execution",
      "summary": "Researchers introduced Agentic Path Maps, a framework addressing how autonomous agents communicate decision trade-offs on underspecified tasks. Instead of interrupting users during execution, the system visualizes impactful, unexplored alternative paths for post-execution user inspection and review.",
      "key_facts": [
        "Distills a design space for post-execution agent decision communication.",
        "Avoids constant mid-task user interruptions during underspecified agent runs.",
        "Exposes impactful and unexplored decision branches for human review."
      ],
      "design_implication": "An agent review screen usually collapses complex multi-step runs into a single linear result, hiding critical trade-offs made along the way. Visualizing discarded decision forks transforms agent oversight from binary approval into an inspectable map where people can evaluate alternatives without enduring endless clarifying prompts. Start prototyping post-run summary views that highlight the two most consequential decision forks the agent rejected.",
      "implication_lens": "pattern",
      "visual_need": "illustrate",
      "source_name": "arXiv cs.HC",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04183",
      "source_date": "2026-11-04T05:00:00.000Z",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Agentic Path Maps communicate impactful, unexplored decision alternatives after execution instead of interrupting users.",
          "quote": "However, interrupting the user for clarification on all such decisions is untenable. How might agents communicate impactful, but unexplored, paths to the user after execution? To investigate, we first distill a design space"
        }
      ],
